# Multi-tenancy

This is a shared-schema, row-level multi-tenant app: every tenant ("company") lives in the
same MySQL database, distinguished by a `company_id` column. There is no schema/DB-per-tenant
isolation — the app layer is the only thing enforcing tenant boundaries. Get any of the rules
below wrong and either (a) two companies' data silently collides, or (b) one company can read or
write another company's data.

## Tenant context

- `AuthContext.getCurrentCompanyId()` / `getCurrentUserRole()` / `getCurrentEmployee()` resolve
  the caller's tenant from the JWT `companyId` claim.
- `SUPER_ADMIN` is the **only** role allowed to bypass a tenant check. Never let a plain `ADMIN`
  (or any other role) skip the company comparison — an admin is scoped to their own company,
  same as everyone else. (`EmployeeLeaveController.validateSelfAccess` and `UserService`'s
  admin-reset methods used to get this wrong by treating `ADMIN` like `SUPER_ADMIN`.)
- A user can belong to multiple companies (`AuthService.switchCompany`) via separate `Employee`
  rows — one user, one row per company membership.

## Rule 1 — every tenant-owned table needs a DIRECT `company_id` column

Don't rely on a join through a parent for tenant scoping, even when the parent is only one hop
away (e.g. `employee_id` → `employees.company_id`). Two reasons:
- You can't put a MySQL composite `UNIQUE` constraint across a join — only across columns in the
  same table (see Rule 2).
- Every guard/service check that walks the join is a chance to forget the check, or for the join
  to be null (e.g. the parent FK is nullable) and silently pass.

If a table only reaches its company transitively today, add the direct column and backfill it —
see the migration template below (`contracts`, `employee_residence_permits`, `payroll`,
`employee_loans`, `loan_sequence`, and `orders` were all fixed this way).

## Rule 2 — unique business codes must be `UNIQUE(company_id, code)`, never a bare global unique

A generated code (invoice number, order number, SKU, employee number, etc.) that's globally
unique will eventually collide, because sequence generators in this codebase reset **per
company** (see Rule 4) — Company A's and Company B's first invoice can both legitimately be
`INV-1000`. If the DB constraint is a bare `UNIQUE` on the code column alone, the second
company's insert throws a false "already exists" error.

Fix template — see any of `V20260615__department_code_unique_per_company.sql`,
`V20260659__payment_code_unique_per_company.sql`, `V20260664__invoice_id_unique_per_company.sql`
(and the rest of the `V202606{64-73}` series) for the exact idempotent pattern: look up the
existing single-column unique index dynamically via `information_schema.STATISTICS` (auto-generated
Hibernate index names vary by environment — don't hardcode them), drop it, add
`UNIQUE (company_id, <code>)`.

If the table doesn't have a direct `company_id` yet, apply Rule 1 first — see
`V20260674__contract_company_scope.sql` through `V20260679__orders_company_scope.sql` for the
combined "add column + backfill + rescope constraint" template.

**Watch for a fix that looks applied but isn't**: `credit_notes` had the correct composite
constraint in code (`CreditNote`'s `@Table(uniqueConstraints = ...)`) AND a leftover global
single-column unique index in the live database from before the fix — the leftover index alone
still caused collisions even though the "right" constraint was also present. When retrofitting an
existing unique column, always confirm the *old* index is actually dropped, not just that the new
one was added.

## Rule 3 — every fetch-by-id must verify tenant ownership before use

`repository.findById(id)` (or `findByCode`/`findByEmail`/etc.) returns whatever row matches,
regardless of which company it belongs to. Every service method that reads, mutates, or deletes
a row fetched this way must check the row belongs to the caller's company before using it.

Two accepted idioms — pick whichever fits the entity:

1. **`EmployeeAccessGuard`** (`security/guard/EmployeeAccessGuard.java`) — for anything reachable
   through `Employee`. `assertCanRead(employee, module)` / `assertCanWrite(employee, module)` /
   `assertCanWrite(employee, module, action)`. Handles SUPER_ADMIN bypass and VIEW_OWN/VIEW_ALL
   ownership logic so you don't reimplement it. See `EmployeeCompensationService`,
   `EmployeeBankDetailsService`.
2. **Private `assertSameTenant(entity)` helper** — for entities with their own direct
   `company_id`. Compares `authContext.getCurrentCompanyId()` to `entity.getCompany().getId()`,
   SUPER_ADMIN bypasses, throws `AccessDeniedException` otherwise. See `PassportService`,
   `ResidencePermitService`, `ContractService`, `EmployeeLoanService`, or the `assertOrderInTenant`
   /`assertInvoiceInTenant`-style helpers already used per-module in finance/inventory/purchase
   services.

If a request body or path variable carries a `companyId` (e.g. `/api/companies/{companyId}/...`),
**never trust it as-is** — compare it against `authContext.getCurrentCompanyId()` (SUPER_ADMIN
bypasses) before using it to scope a query or fetch. `@PreAuthorize`/`@RequiresPermission` in this
codebase check module/action permission only — they do **not** check tenant, so a path-variable
`companyId` sails straight through unless the service checks it explicitly.

## Rule 4 — sequence/code generators must be keyed by `companyId`

`DocumentSequenceService.generateNext(prefix)` and `nextEmployeeNo(companyId)` already key their
counter by `companyId + "_" + prefix` — copy this pattern for any new generated code. Keying the
*counter* by company is necessary but not sufficient on its own — the DB constraint (Rule 2) must
also be company-scoped, or the correctly-reset-per-company codes will still collide against a
global unique index.

## Checklist — run through this before shipping anything touching tenant-scoped data

- [ ] New entity holding tenant data has a **direct** `company_id` column (not just reachable via a parent join).
- [ ] Any `@Column(unique = true)` or `@UniqueConstraint` on a business code includes `company_id` in the same constraint.
- [ ] Any new Flyway migration adding a unique index on a code/name column is composite with `company_id`.
- [ ] Any service method that does `repository.findById(id)` (or `findByCode`/`findByEmail`/etc.) follows up with a tenant check — `EmployeeAccessGuard` or a `assertSameTenant`-style helper — before reading, mutating, or deleting.
- [ ] Any bypass of a tenant check is gated on `SUPER_ADMIN` specifically, never a general `isAdmin()`/`ADMIN` role check.
- [ ] Any generated business code's counter is keyed by `companyId` (see `DocumentSequenceService`).
- [ ] List/search endpoints filter by the caller's company at the query level, not just in-memory after fetch.
- [ ] If a client request body or path variable includes a `companyId`, it's validated against `auth.getCurrentCompanyId()`, never trusted as-is.
